  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 1155 AM MDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nThe long term period will feature a warming and drying trend as mid-\nlevel heights rise over our region and the bulk of any forcing for \nascent remain to the north or west of our area. Tuesday will feature \na slight chance of late day showers and storms as moisture remains \ntrapped underneath the ridge and we remain in SW flow pattern (PWAT \naround the 70-80th percentile), however coverage is expected to be \nlower relative to today and Monday. High temperatures will again \nremain close to climatology, with highs in the 80s east of the \nLaramie Range and mainly 70s out west. \n\nWe begin to heat up on Wednesday and Thursday as the ridge axis \nbecomes focused closer to the Northern Great Plains. This will turn \nour attention to potential fire weather concerns as long-range \nensemble guidance has signals for gusty winds and daytime RH values \nranging from 15-25% especially north of the I-80 corridor. Highs \nwill peak in the upper 70s to near 80 degrees west of the Laramie \nRange on both days, with much of the Nebraska Panhandle soaring into \nthe low to mid 90s, which is still a few degrees short of record \nhighs. \n\nAs we end the work week and head into the weekend, the ridge begins \nto amplify and establish itself over the Central and Southern Great \nPlains, resulting in a containing trend of above-average temperatures \nand primarily dry conditions. There are some signals of an \nadditional push of monsoonal moisture on Saturday and Sunday, \nhowever confidence isn't high enough at this time to mention any \nwidespread precipitation for the region.",
